Approach: Sliding Window with Hash Map. The solution uses a sliding window approach with a hash map to track the last occurrence of each character. This allows efficient updates to the window's start position when duplicates are found, ensuring an optimal solution.

Algorithm: Initialize a hash map to store the last index of each character. Initialize two pointers, left and right, to represent the current window's start and end. Iterate through the string with the right pointer. If the current character has been seen and its last index is greater than or equal to the left pointer, update the left pointer to be one position after the last occurrence of the current character. Update the last occurrence of the current character in the hash map. Update the maximum length of the substring found so far.

Correctness: The algorithm ensures that the window always contains unique characters by adjusting the left pointer whenever a duplicate is encountered. This guarantees that the window is valid and the maximum length is tracked correctly.

Time: O(n). Space: O(min(m, n)).

