Constraints: The number of nodes in the list is n. | 1 <= k <= n <= 5000 | 0 <= Node.val <= 1000 | Follow-up: Can you solve the problem in O(1) extra memory space?

Approach: Iterative Reversal with Group Tracking. The solution iteratively reverses groups of k nodes in the linked list. It uses pointers to track the start and end of each group, reverses the group, and links it back to the previous part of the list. This approach ensures that the reversal is done in-place and handles edge cases such as when the number of nodes is not a multiple of k.

Algorithm: Initialize a dummy node to handle edge cases where the head might change. Use a pointer to track the current group's start. Iterate through the list, checking if there are at least k nodes remaining. For each group of k nodes, reverse them and link the reversed group to the previous part of the list. If the number of nodes is not a multiple of k, leave the remaining nodes as-is. Return the new head of the modified list.

Correctness: The algorithm correctly reverses each group of k nodes by maintaining pointers to the start and end of each group. It ensures that the reversed group is properly linked to the previous part of the list, and any remaining nodes are left untouched. This guarantees that the final list meets the problem's requirements.

Time: O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the linked list. Each node is visited once during the traversal and reversal.. Space: O(1), as the algorithm uses a constant amount of extra space for pointers and does not use any additional data structures..

Java solution:
class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseKGroup(ListNode head, int k) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;
        ListNode prev = dummy;
        ListNode curr = head;
        int count = 0;

        while (curr != null) {
            count++;
            if (count % k == 0) {
                prev = reverseGroup(prev, curr);
                curr = prev.next;
            } else {
                curr = curr.next;
            }
        }
        return dummy.next;
    }

    private ListNode reverseGroup(ListNode prev, ListNode curr) {
        ListNode last = prev.next;
        ListNode currNext = null;
        ListNode next = null;

        while (curr != null) {
            next = curr.next;
            curr.next = currNext;
            currNext = curr;
            curr = next;
        }

        last.next = currNext;
        prev.next = currNext;
        return last;
    }
}

