Approach: Dynamic Programming with Obstacle Handling. We use dynamic programming to calculate the number of unique paths from the top-left to the bottom-right of a grid, avoiding obstacles. The number of paths to a cell is the sum of the paths from the cell above and the cell to the left, unless the cell is an obstacle.

Algorithm: Initialize a DP table with the same dimensions as the grid. If the starting cell (0,0) is an obstacle, return 0. Set the starting cell's DP value to 1 if it is not an obstacle. Iterate through the grid, updating the DP table as follows:    If the current cell is an obstacle, set its DP value to 0.    Otherwise, set DP[i][j] = DP[i-1][j] + DP[i][j-1]. Return the value in the bottom-right cell of the DP table.

Correctness: The DP approach correctly accumulates the number of paths to each cell by considering only valid moves (down or right). Obstacles are handled by setting their DP value to 0, which ensures they are not counted in any path.

Time: O(m * n). Space: O(m * n).

Java solution:
class Solution {
    public int uniquePathsWithObstacles(int[][] obstacleGrid) {
        int m = obstacleGrid.length;
        int n = obstacleGrid[0].length;
        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];

        if (obstacleGrid[0][0] == 1) return 0;
        dp[0][0] = 1;

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (obstacleGrid[i][j] == 1) {
                    dp[i][j] = 0;
                } else if (i == 0 && j == 0) {
                    dp[i][j] = 1;
                } else {
                    int up = (i > 0) ? dp[i - 1][j] : 0;
                    int left = (j > 0) ? dp[i][j - 1] : 0;
                    dp[i][j] = up + left;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m - 1][n - 1];
    }
}

