Approach: Dynamic Programming with Iteration. The problem can be solved using dynamic programming. The number of ways to reach step n is the sum of the ways to reach step n-1 and step n-2, as you can arrive at n by taking 1 step from n-1 or 2 steps from n-2. This is similar to the Fibonacci sequence.

Algorithm: Initialize variables a = 1 (ways to reach step 1), b = 2 (ways to reach step 2). Iterate from 3 to n, updating a and b as a = b, b = a + b. Return b as the result for n.

Correctness: The recurrence relation is correct because each step can be reached by either taking 1 step from the previous step or 2 steps from the step before that. This leads to the Fibonacci sequence, where the nth term represents the number of ways to reach the nth step.

Time: O(n). Space: O(1).

Java solution:
class Solution {
    public int climbStairs(int n) {
        if (n == 1) return 1;
        if (n == 2) return 2;
        int a = 1, b = 2;
        for (int i = 3; i <= n; i++) {
            int temp = b;
            b = a + b;
            a = temp;
        }
        return b;
    }
}

