Approach: Backtracking with Lexicographical Order. The solution uses backtracking to generate all combinations of k numbers from 1 to n. It ensures combinations are built in lexicographical order to avoid duplicates.

Algorithm: Initialize an empty list to store the result. Define a helper function that builds combinations recursively. In the helper function, if the current combination size reaches k, add it to the result. Otherwise, iterate from the current starting number to n, and for each number, add it to the current combination and recursively call the helper function with the next starting number. After the recursive call, remove the last number to backtrack.

Correctness: The algorithm ensures that each combination is built in lexicographical order, which prevents duplicates. By starting the next iteration from the current number, we ensure that combinations are generated in a way that avoids permutations of the same set.

Time: O(C(n, k) * k). Space: O(C(n, k) * k).

Java solution:
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combine(int n, int k) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        backtrack(result, new ArrayList<>(), 1, n, k);
        return result;
    }

    private void backtrack(List<List<Integer>> result, List<Integer> current, int start, int n, int k) {
        if (current.size() == k) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }
        for (int i = start; i <= n; i++) {
            current.add(i);
            backtrack(result, current, i + 1, n, k);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}

