LeetCode 92: Reverse Linked List II

Difficulty: Medium

Problem: Given the head of a singly linked list and two integers left and right where left <= right, reverse the nodes of the list from position left to position right, and return the reversed list.

Example 1:

Input: head = [1,2,3,4,5], left = 2, right = 4
Output: [1,4,3,2,5]

Example 2:

Input: head = [5], left = 1, right = 1
Output: [5]

Constraints:

 The number of nodes in the list is n.
 1 <= n <= 500
 -500 <= Node.val <= 500
 1 <= left <= right <= n

Follow up: Could you do it in one pass?

Constraints: The number of nodes in the list is n. | 1 <= n <= 500 | -500 <= Node.val <= 500 | 1 <= left <= right <= n

Approach: Iterative Reversal with Pointers. The solution reverses the sublist from position left to right by first identifying the nodes at these positions, then reversing the sublist in between, and reconnecting the reversed part to the rest of the list.

Algorithm: Handle the edge case where left == right, return the head as no reversal is needed. Create a dummy node to simplify edge cases (e.g., when reversing starts from the head). Traverse to the node just before the left position, keeping track of the previous node. Reverse the sublist from left to right by adjusting the next pointers of the nodes in this range. Reconnect the reversed sublist to the rest of the list. Return the new head of the modified list.

Correctness: The algorithm correctly identifies the sublist to reverse, reverses it in-place, and reconnects the segments. The dummy node ensures that edge cases like reversing from the head are handled seamlessly.

Time: O(n). Space: O(1).

Java solution:
class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseBetween(ListNode head, int left, int right) {
        if (left == right) return head;
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;
        ListNode prev = dummy;
        for (int i = 0; i < left - 1; i++) {
            prev = prev.next;
        }
        ListNode current = prev.next;
        ListNode nextNode = null;
        ListNode prev2 = null;
        for (int i = 0; i <= right - left; i++) {
            nextNode = current.next;
            current.next = prev2;
            prev2 = current;
            current = nextNode;
        }
        prev.next.next = prev2;
        prev.next = current;
        return dummy.next;
    }
}

